---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import { site, isTodo } from '../data/site';

const people = site.people.filter((p) => !isTodo(p.name));
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Loomwise is a European company building process automation for small and mid-sized businesses and skilled trades."
>
  <section class="section weave">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Why Loomwise</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        In 1804 Joseph-Marie Jacquard put a chain of punched cards on a loom.
        The pattern stopped living in the weaver’s memory and became something
        you could write down, hand to someone else, and run again tomorrow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        It was the first programmable machine. The punched card went from the
        loom to the census, to the tabulator, to the computer — the whole line
        starts in a workshop, on a tool used by a trade.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the idea we work from. Automation at its best does not replace
        the craft; it writes down the repetitive part so the skilled part gets
        the time. The weaver still decides what to make.
      </p>

      <h2>What we believe</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Small companies deserve real engineering.</strong> The
        automation industry sells to enterprises because that is where the
        budgets are. A fifty-person manufacturer has the same repetitive
        administrative load and none of the internal capacity to fix it. That is
        a solvable problem, not a market segment to skip.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Say when something is not worth automating.</strong> Some
        processes should be simplified, not automated, and some should be left
        alone. A supplier who never says that is selling hours rather than
        outcomes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compliance is part of the build, not a document at the end.</strong>
        The GDPR and the EU AI Act shape architecture decisions. Retrofitting
        them costs more than designing for them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>No lock-in.</strong> Every engagement ends with documentation
        good enough for someone else to take over. If the only reason you stay
        is that leaving is painful, we have built the wrong thing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {
    people.length > 0 && (
      <section class="section section--sunk">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">People</p>
          <h2>Who you will be working with</h2>
          <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
            {people.map((p) => (
              <article class="card">
                <h3>{p.name}</h3>
                <p class="role">{p.role}</p>
                <p>{p.bio}</p>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    )
  }

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The company</h2>
      <p>
        {site.legalName} is a Polish limited liability company, registered in
        Poland and operating across the European Union. Working from Poland with
        EU-wide delivery is a deliberate choice: it keeps our cost base
        realistic for the size of company we serve, and it keeps every
        engagement inside a single legal and data-protection regime.
      </p>
      <p>
        Full registration details are on the <a href="/imprint/">imprint page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTA />
</Base>

<style>
  .role {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--copper);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
